In this lesson we'll look at ratios of lengths, and how a single number, called the scale factor, links every length on one shape to the matching length on a similar shape. The key idea is simple: if two shapes are mathematically similar, then every length on the smaller one gets multiplied by the same number to give the corresponding length on the larger one. So we say the lengths are in a direct ratio, where the larger length stands to the smaller one as that scale factor stands to one. You see this all the time. A map scale of one to fifty thousand means every centimetre on the map is fifty thousand centimetres, or five hundred metres, in real life. A model plane at one to forty-eight is forty-eight times smaller in every direction. And a photocopier set to one hundred and forty percent is multiplying every length by one point four. A quick check, a twelve centimetre pencil at a scale of one to three becomes a thirty-six centimetre pencil model. Now let's see it in action.